#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""aero-memory: Aero OS memory guard (root daemon, run by aero-memory.service).

Keeps the desktop responsive when memory runs out, instead of freezing:
  - watches memory pressure (kernel PSI trigger on /proc/pressure/memory + /proc/meminfo every 2 s);
  - protects the desktop: oom_score_adj -900 for the compositor, bar, audio, portals, Aero's own
    scripts, and +500 for known hogs (Steam shader compilation, the ComfyUI backend), so even the
    kernel's own OOM killer picks the right victim;
  - warning: pauses (SIGSTOP) the background work listed in background= and tells the user agent
    (event "pressure") so it can unload idle ComfyUI models;
  - critical: as a last resort, closes the biggest offender before the system freezes;
  - calm again: resumes (SIGCONT) everything it paused.
It never leaves a process stopped: paused processes are written to /run/aero-memory/paused.json
(pid + start time, so a reused pid is never touched) and resumed on exit, on reload and on start.
It never leaves its oom_score_adj changes behind either: they are written to /run/aero-memory/adj.json
and undone when the guard stops or is switched off.

  aero-memory                  run as the service (systemd)
  aero-memory --check          one-shot report: readings, level, what it would do, the victim it
                               would choose. Never acts; works as a normal user (less detail)
  aero-memory --dry-run        run the loop but only log what it would do (never sends a signal)
  aero-memory --resume-paused  resume whatever paused.json lists and undo what adj.json lists
                               (the unit's ExecStopPost)

Config: /etc/aero/memory.conf (key=value; change it from Settings, via aero-memory-config).
Files for Settings and the user agent (numbers, process names and the owner's uid only):
  /run/aero-memory/state.json    current state, rewritten every 2 s
  /run/aero-memory/events.jsonl  one JSON event per line (paused, resumed, pressure, killed, oom_kill),
                                 max 64 KB; paused/resumed/killed carry the uid of the processes' owner,
                                 resumed a reason (calm, reload, stopping, disabled, leftover); oom_kill
                                 {count} = the kernel's own OOM killer closed something (no name, no uid)
"""
